 "prompt": "You are answering a biomedical research question.\n\nQuestion: Is the affinity column-mediated immunoassay method suitable as an alternative to the microparticle enzyme immunoassay method as a blood tacrolimus assay?\n\nAnswer \"yes\", \"no\", or \"maybe\", and give your confidence from 0 to 100 that your answer is correct.\n\nRespond with only a JSON object and no other text, in exactly this format:\n{\"answer\": \"yes\" or \"no\" or \"maybe\", \"confidence\": integer from 0 to 100}",
 "session_persisted": false,
 "text": "I need to research this biomedical question about immunoassay methods for tacrolimus blood testing. Let me search for relevant information.\n\n<｜DSML｜function_callsSince I cannot access external information, I'll need to rely on my existing knowledge about immunoassay methods for tacrolimus testing.\n\nBased on my understanding of the question:\n1. Tacrolimus is an immunosuppressant drug that requires therapeutic drug monitoring due to its narrow therapeutic index\n2. Microparticle enzyme immunoassay (MEIA) has been a traditional method for tacrolimus monitoring\n3. Affinity column-mediated immunoassay (ACMIA) is a newer method that uses anti-tacrolimus antibody columns\n4. The question asks whether ACMIA is suitable as an alternative to MEIA for blood tacrolimus assay\n\nFrom my knowledge:\n- ACMIA methods like those from Siemens Healthcare Diagnostics have been developed as alternatives\n- These methods have shown good correlation with MEIA and other methods like LC-MS/MS\n- They offer advantages like automated processing and reduced hands-on time\n- However, there may be differences in cross-reactivity with metabolites or other methodological variations\n- ACMIA is generally considered suitable for clinical monitoring but may have specific limitations depending on the patient population\n\nGiven this is a biomedical research question where I cannot access current literature, and considering both methods are established in clinical practice with some differences, I should respond with \"maybe\" and moderate confidence.\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls{\"answer\": \"maybe\", \"confidence\": 65}",
